    "answer": "Here is my analysis and proposed solutions.\n\n---\n\n## Frame\n\n**Context:** Visual Relay is a .NET cross-platform desktop app (Avalonia) with a substantial xUnit test suite (~100+ test files, all in a single `VisualRelay.Tests` project). The test project references `Avalonia.Headless.XUnit` for GUI tests and covers all source + tool projects.\n\n**Current bottleneck:** Both `TestRunner.RunAsync` and `CheckCommand.RunWatchedTestsAsync` invoke `dotnet test` with the **`-m:1`** flag (MaxCpuCount=1), which forces MSBuild/test-execution to run strictly sequentially \u2014 one test at a time. Although xUnit's own `xunit.runner.json` already has `\"parallelizeTestCollections\": true`, the `-m:1` flag suppresses parallelism at the CLI level.\n\n**Goal:** Speed up the test suite by enabling parallel test execution while preserving correctness (no flaky tests from shared-state contention, sandbox conflicts, or Avalonia UI thread requirements).\n\n---\n\n## Solution Options\n\n### 1. Bump `-m` to `Environment.ProcessorCount` (or remove `-m` entirely)\nReplace `-m:1` with `-m:<n>` where `n` = available logical cores, or simply drop `-m` so `dotnet test` defaults to `ProcessorCount`. This is the **simplest change** \u2014 two lines in `TestRunner.cs` and `CheckCommand.cs`. The risk is that some tests share mutable state (filesystem fixtures, sandbox processes, backend lifecycle) and may become flaky when run concurrently. Some Avalonia headless tests may also assume a single UI thread.\n\n### 2. Structured parallelism via multiple test projects\nSplit the monolithic `VisualRelay.Tests` project into **~3\u20134 focused test projects** (e.g., `VisualRelay.Core.Tests`, `VisualRelay.App.Tests`, `VisualRelay.Cli.Tests`), each with its own `xunit.runner.json` and isolated fixture state. Then run them concurrently with separate `dotnet test` invocations (no `-m` flag needed). This eliminates intra-project state bleed and lets the CLI orchestrate them in parallel. The trade-off is more project maintenance, longer build times per project, and a refactor of shared test infrastructure.\n\n### 3. Enable `dotnet test` parallelism (`-m` auto) + selectively mark serial-only tests\nKeep the single project but replace `-m:1` with auto-parallelism (remove the flag). Then identify tests that cannot run concurrently (e.g., those that start a backend process, write to the same temp files, or depend on Avalonia's single-threaded dispatcher) and annotate them with `[CollectionDefinition(DisableParallelization = true)]` or `[Collection(\"Serial\")]`. This is a middle-ground: simpler than splitting projects but requires a one-time audit and annotation of ~10\u201320 serial-required tests.\n\n---\n\n```json\n{\n  \"summary\": \"The test suite runs entirely sequentially via `dotnet test -m:1` in `TestRunner.cs` and `CheckCommand.cs`, despite xUnit already being configured for parallel collections (`parallelizeTestCollections: true`). The bottleneck is the `-m:1` flag. Options: 1) simply bump/remove `-m` (minimal change, possible flaky tests), 2) split into multiple test projects run concurrently (clean isolation, more project overhead), 3) remove `-m:1` and selectively mark serial-only tests with xUnit collections (middle ground, requires one-time audit).\",\n  \"options\": [\n    \"Bump `-m:1` to `-m:<ProcessorCount>` (or drop it) in TestRunner.cs + CheckCommand.cs \u2014 simplest change, 2 lines; risk of flaky tests from shared state or Avalonia UI thread contention.\",\n    \"Split the monolithic test project into ~3-4 focused projects (Core.Tests, App.Tests, Cli.Tests, etc.) and run each with a concurrent `dotnet test` call \u2014 cleanest isolation but more project maintenance and shared-test-infra refactoring.\",\n    \"Remove `-m:1` and annotate serial-required tests with `[Collection(\\\"Serial\\\")]` \u2014 balances simplicity with correctness; requires a one-time audit to identify tests that conflict under parallelism.\"\n  ]\n}\n```",
